  "analyze": {
    "calibration": {
      "planned": "C (hygiene: push, gitleaks, sweep) and D (infrastructure: MCP server, evidence eval) — his own list.",
      "delivered": "P1 hygiene in full. P2 delivered the evidence half of D, RESCOPED after A.0 found the plan's own description was a category error, and the MCP server DEFERRED. P3 and P4 were owner layout corrections that did not exist when the session opened. P5 (the bundle guard) came out of a defect I shipped.",
      "estimate_error": "I sized D as one batch. A.0 dissolved half of it before any code was written — /inspect-eval is an LLM harness and \"every built tile cites evidence\" is a deterministic property, so it belonged in npm run check, not in an eval. The MCP server was deferred on its own merits: it serves OUR workflow, not his game, and his roadmap says finish the zone first. Deferring it was right; the precondition (a silently stale index) was fixed anyway, so the server is now a small safe addition whenever he wants it.",
      "what_i_got_right": "Probing before planning. A.0 found five things that changed the work: the gitleaks finding was a model id, realm.db was 12 records behind, the sweep as described would have deleted the running session, the eval was a category error, and no MCP infrastructure existed at all. Every one of those would have been a wasted build.",
      "what_i_got_wrong": "I verified data and showed a Python render, then told him it was done without ever loading the game. He saw a scrambled zone. Five guards were green the whole time."
    },
    "retrospective": {
      "biggest_win": "Making the citation a REQUIRED PARAMETER rather than a lint over comments. It caught five dynamically-named tiles a regex sweep could not see, and it caught ME inventing 11 record ids within minutes of the guard existing — the exact failure Grok predicted at severity 10 during PLAN.",
      "biggest_miss": "The stale bundle. Every guard checked the sources against each other; none checked that the artifact the player loads was built from them. \"All green\" and \"the game is garbage\" were compatible states. Fixed with a sixth guard, but it should not have taken him asking what the hell had happened.",
      "method": "Three Council proposals were rejected with MEASUREMENT rather than opinion — SSIM, my own hex-in-palette fallback, and Gemini's new strength field. The colour data (median 27.5, nearest step often an unrelated palette) said no threshold was supportable, so none was invented. Shipping no number was the honest result.",
      "owner_loop": "Four corrections across two rounds, and the DATABASE ALREADY CONTAINED THREE OF THEM in plain prose — the smithy-wall relationship, the open field, and the railed frontage. The build kept losing to fills authored before the record existed. That is now the clearest structural gap in the project: nothing asks whether the built world still agrees with the records that describe it.",
      "love_it_gate": "Not reached, and correctly so — the zone is still being corrected. But the session SHOWED him work five times and every showing produced a correction that improved it, which is what 13.1 asks for."
    }
  }
